@Screen elements

!b;Backgrounds
!t;Tiles
!w;Windows
!i;Indicators
!x;Bits
!n;Numbers
!o;Others

:b;Backgrounds
In the tile editor menu, you can specify the character and colour, and if
they should be shown. Whichever is not shown will display whatever was
previously shown on the display (this can be used for popup menus that only
take up a part of the screen).

(Transparent cells in the main screen will show the default defined in the
program memory if defined, or will be treated as opaque otherwise.)

In the F1 menu, "Opaque" means both, "Shadow" means the colour only, and
"Transparent" means neither.

:t;Tiles
Displays a tile from the board.

"Suppress overlay" will cause it to always display the main layer if this
is selected; if not, then it will display the overlay if there is a visible
overlay tile at that position in the board, or the main layer if it is a
transparent overlay or if the light radius hides the overlay.

If "Use defaults if out of range" is selected, then the default character
and colour are displayed if the camera views beyond the board edges. If not
selected, it will display the board tile of the nearest edge.

"Show border" causes it to display the border character and colour defined
for this screen if it is outside of the board grid but adjacent to it.

In the F1 menu, "Light" suppresses the overlay and "Dark" displays it (if
there is an overlay).

:w;Windows
There are two kind of windows, being text windows and item windows.

A text window tile is specified by the default character, and the primary
and secondary colours.

The default character is used for the top and bottom of the text window,
beyond the ends of the text; the window flags can be used to specify if
they appear only once at each end or everywhere with no text.

The primary colour is used for normal text, while the secondary colour is
used for centered text and links. The window definition can override these.

Item window tiles have multiple kinds, as follows:

þ Placeholder: Used to display individual item slots in a item menu. The
details are defined by the window definition.

þ Element: Displays the element associated with the item in a specified
slot in a specified inventory list. If this tile is inside of a item slot
in a item menu, then the parameter is ignored and it will use that slot
in the current inventory list instead.

þ Select field: Define a field to be displayed by the following "Numbers"
commands which are defined as context-specific; they will be displayed
for the currently selected item. They are defined in the same way as in
variable property lists, except that you can also specify which of the
four border characters of the screen to use.

þ Show field: (not implemented yet)

þ Flags: Displays the character if one of the eight custom flags is set for
the current item slot (either the one that the cursor is on, or the one
that corresponds to that position on the screen), or blank otherwise.

:i;Indicators
An indicator has a character code to display if it is "set"; if the
indicator is "clear" then a blank space is displayed (unless you set in
the screen info menu to use the border characters instead).

þ Board user data - Displayed if the board's user data field is nonzero.

þ Cursor - In the main game, displayed if the player's X or Y coordinate
is lined up with the indicator. In a text window, displayed if the cursor
is at the current line.

þ Scroll - Displayed if more of the board (or text, in the case of a text
window) is beyond the nearest edge of the viewport.

þ Exits - Displayed if the specified exit is nonzero.

þ User - Displayed if the corresponding user flag is set in the board info
for the current board.

:x;Bits
This means the bits of one of the first four status variables A to D, where
bits 0 to 31 are A, 32 to 63 are B, etc.

The character is displayed if that bit is set (0 means the low bit), or a
blank space is displayed if that bit is clear.

:n;Numbers
Numbers will display a selected numeric value, using a selected numeric
format (the numeric format numbers being numbered 0 to 15).
!-numform;Numeric formats

The numeric value to be displayed can be a user-defined status variable
from (A) to (Z), or one of the following special numeric variables:

þ Player X/Y - The zero-based coordinates of the player (stat 1 on the
current board), where the northwest corner is (0,0).

þ Camera X/Y - The camera position.

þ Scroll % - In a text window, the percentage scroll from the top (0%) to
the bottom (100%).

þ Line number - In a text window, the line number where the cursor position
is currently at.

þ Line count - In a text window, the number of lines of text (including
those which are not currently visible).

þ Cur. board - The current board number. (You can use a numeric format
which is defined to display the name of the board.)

þ Exits - The board number of the exits from the current board.

þ Width/Height - The size of the current board.

þ User - The user data of the current board.

þ Context-specific - The number to be displayed depends on the context.
This is mainly used for inventory menus.

In addition to which variable to display, the position (also 0 to 15)
must also be selected; the meaning of the positions depends on the
definitions of the numeric formats.

:o;Others
Memory means to display the contents of a single 16-bit cell of program
memory. You must specify the address. The low byte of the data of that
memory cell will decide the character to display, and the high byte will
decide the colour to display.

